---
import Site from '../layouts/Site.astro';
import PaymentFlow from '../components/PaymentFlow.astro';
import { VERIFIED_ON, VERIFIED_ON_ISO } from '../content/proof';
import { paths } from '../i18n/routes';

// Every claim on this page is re-checked at the source before each release, and
// the date below changes with it. Spec v5, section 7.
const verifiedOn = VERIFIED_ON.en;

const wording = [
	{ avoid: 'escrow', use: 'the payment is held by the platform until delivery is confirmed' },
	{ avoid: 'we hold the funds in custody', use: 'the transfer to the seller leaves after confirmation' },
	{ avoid: 'wallet', use: 'a balance shown in the app that reflects real transfers' },
	{ avoid: 'trust account', use: 'a charge on the platform, then a separate transfer' },
];

const faq = [
	{
		q: 'Does Stripe offer escrow?',
		a: 'No. Escrow is a regulated activity, not a product feature, and "Escrow services" appears on Stripe\'s own list of restricted businesses under financial services.',
	},
	{
		q: 'What do you build instead?',
		a: 'A charge on the platform and a separate transfer, released when delivery is confirmed. Stripe documents the pattern as separate charges and transfers, and it gets approved.',
	},
	{
		q: 'Why was my Stripe application rejected without an explanation?',
		a: 'If an account application says you hold money on behalf of other people, it goes to the risk team rather than onboarding. Risk decisions are not explained.',
	},
];

const title = 'Stripe does not do escrow. What to use instead | Quick Flow';
const description =
	'Escrow is a legal term, and Stripe lists escrow services as a restricted business. What gets approved is a charge on the platform and a separate transfer.';

const schema = {
	'@context': 'https://schema.org',
	'@type': 'FAQPage',
	mainEntity: faq.map((f) => ({
		'@type': 'Question',
		name: f.q,
		acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: f.a },
	})),
};
---

<Site lang="en" title={title} description={description} dateModified={VERIFIED_ON_ISO}>
	<script is:inline type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(schema)} />

	<!-- 1. Recognition. The reader arrived here from a rejection email. -->
	<section class="bleed pt-12 md:pt-16">
		<div class="flex flex-wrap items-baseline justify-between gap-x-10 gap-y-3">
			<p class="spec">Marketplace payments</p>
			<span class="stamp">Sources checked {verifiedOn}</span>
		</div>

		<h1 class="display-xl mt-8 max-w-[16ch]">
			Stripe does not do <span class="strike">escrow</span>. That is why you were rejected.
		</h1>

		<p class="lead mt-9 max-w-[56ch] text-lg">
			You described a flow where the money sits somewhere until delivery, you used the word escrow,
			and the answer came back rejected with no explanation. The thing you want can be built, and
			it gets approved. It is just not called that, and it is not built that way.
		</p>
	</section>

	<!-- 2. What escrow actually is. Facts with the source attached. -->
	<section class="bleed mt-32" aria-labelledby="what">
		<div class="hair grid gap-8 pt-10 md:grid-cols-12 md:pt-14">
			<div class="md:col-span-5">
				<p class="spec">Facts, not opinions</p>
				<h2 id="what" class="display-lg mt-6">Escrow is a legal term.</h2>
			</div>
			<div class="md:col-span-6 md:col-start-7">
				<p class="prose-body">
					Escrow means a neutral third party holds money on behalf of two parties under
					pre-agreed rules. It is a regulated activity, not a product feature.
				</p>
				<p class="prose-body mt-5">
					Stripe has no such product. More than that, "Escrow services" appears explicitly on their
					list of restricted businesses, under financial services. Checked on{' '}
					<a
						href="https://stripe.com/legal/restricted-businesses"
						rel="noopener nofollow"
						target="_blank"
						class="link">their restricted businesses page</a
					>, {verifiedOn}.
				</p>
				<p class="prose-body mt-5">
					If an account application says you hold money in custody for other people, the request
					reaches the risk team rather than onboarding. Nobody explains it to you, because risk
					decisions do not come with explanations.
				</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<!-- 3. What you actually want, with the drawing. -->
	<section class="bleed mt-32" aria-labelledby="want">
		<div class="hair grid items-center gap-12 pt-10 lg:grid-cols-12 lg:gap-10 md:pt-14">
			<div class="lg:col-span-5">
				<p class="spec">What you actually want</p>
				<h2 id="want" class="display-lg mt-6">
					The buyer pays now. The seller is paid when the work is done.
				</h2>
				<p class="prose-body mt-7">
					That is not escrow. It is a charge on the platform and a separate transfer, released on
					confirmation. Stripe's own documentation calls it{' '}
					<a
						href="https://docs.stripe.com/connect/separate-charges-and-transfers"
						rel="noopener nofollow"
						target="_blank"
						class="link">separate charges and transfers</a
					>, it is a documented pattern, and it gets approved.
				</p>
			</div>
			<div class="min-w-0 lg:col-span-6 lg:col-start-7">
				<PaymentFlow lang="en" />
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<!-- 4. Why the obvious shortcut is wrong. -->
	<section class="bleed mt-32" aria-labelledby="delay">
		<div class="hair grid gap-8 pt-10 md:grid-cols-12 md:pt-14">
			<h2 id="delay" class="display-md md:col-span-5">
				A payout delay is a timer, not a condition.
			</h2>
			<div class="md:col-span-6 md:col-start-7">
				<p class="prose-body">
					The first solution everyone reaches for is delaying the payout on the seller's connected
					account. It looks like it works, because the money really does arrive later.
				</p>
				<p class="prose-body mt-5">
					Except a timer does not know whether the work was done. If an order is cancelled on day
					two, the money is already in the seller's balance and has to be pulled back out. If the
					seller has withdrawn it, you recover it from their next sale or not at all.
				</p>
				<p class="prose-body mt-5">
					A transfer on confirmation does not have that problem. Until confirmation the money never
					left the platform, and a cancellation is an ordinary refund, on a path Stripe already
					handles.
				</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<!-- 5. The substitution table, on ink. This is the single most useful
	     object on the site: four phrases to delete and four to write. -->
	<section class="band mt-16 md:mt-20" aria-labelledby="application">
		<div class="bleed py-20 md:py-28">
			<p class="spec">The practical part</p>
			<h2 id="application" class="display-lg mt-6 max-w-[22ch]">
				What to write in the account application so it is not rejected.
			</h2>
			<p class="mt-8 max-w-[62ch] text-lg leading-snug">
				Describe the movement of the money, not the legal construction. Who pays, who is paid, when
				each share leaves, and who carries the refund. No terms that sound like a financial
				service.
			</p>

			<div class="mt-12 overflow-x-auto">
				<table class="w-full border-collapse text-left">
					<thead>
						<tr>
							<th class="spec border-b border-[var(--line)] pb-3 pr-6">Instead of</th>
							<th class="spec border-b border-[var(--line)] pb-3">Write</th>
						</tr>
					</thead>
					<tbody>
						{
							wording.map((c) => (
								<tr>
									<td class="strike border-b border-[var(--line-soft)] py-4 pr-6 align-top text-paper/68">
										{c.avoid}
									</td>
									<td class="border-b border-[var(--line-soft)] py-4 align-top">{c.use}</td>
								</tr>
							))
						}
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</div>

			<p class="prose-body mt-10 max-w-[62ch] text-[0.95rem]">
				Add the concrete things the risk team is looking for anyway: what is sold, who sells it,
				what an average order is worth, how long delivery takes, and what happens when it is not
				delivered. A short, precise description passes more easily than a long, careful one.
			</p>
		</div>
	</section>

	<!-- 6. The detail almost nobody knows, and the reason not to build on it. -->
	<section class="bleed mt-16 md:mt-20" aria-labelledby="preview">
		<div class="grid gap-8 rounded-2xl border border-[var(--line)] p-7 md:grid-cols-12 md:p-12">
			<div class="md:col-span-5">
				<p class="spec">A rare detail</p>
				<h2 id="preview" class="display-md mt-6">There is a way of holding funds.</h2>
				<p class="mt-8 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
					<span class="state state-risk">Private preview</span>
					<span class="state">Case by case</span>
				</p>
			</div>
			<div class="md:col-span-6 md:col-start-7">
				<p class="prose-body">
					Stripe describes a feature called funds segregation, which holds the funds of a payment
					in a protected state before the transfer to the connected account. It is in private
					preview, and access is requested through your account manager.
				</p>
				<p class="prose-body mt-5">
					I do not build on it and I would not recommend it as your main plan, because you cannot
					depend on a feature granted case by case. I mention it because it is the only real
					approximation of what people imagine escrow means at Stripe.
				</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<!-- 7. The three questions this page gets asked, written out so they can be
	     read without the page, and so Google can read them too. -->
	<section class="bleed mt-32" aria-labelledby="faq">
		<div class="hair flex items-baseline justify-between gap-6 pt-5">
			<p class="spec">Asked most often</p>
			<span class="spec">03</span>
		</div>
		<h2 id="faq" class="display-lg mt-8 max-w-[24ch]">Three questions, answered in one line each.</h2>

		<dl class="mt-12 flex flex-col">
			{
				faq.map((f) => (
					<div class="hair grid gap-4 py-8 md:grid-cols-12 md:gap-8">
						<dt class="display-md md:col-span-5">{f.q}</dt>
						<dd class="prose-body md:col-span-6 md:col-start-7">{f.a}</dd>
					</div>
				))
			}
		</dl>
	</section>

	<!-- 8. Close, on ink. -->
	<section class="band mt-16 md:mt-20">
		<div class="bleed py-20 md:py-28">
			<div class="grid gap-10 md:grid-cols-12">
				<div class="md:col-span-7">
					<h2 class="display-lg">Send me your flow and I will tell you whether it gets approved.</h2>
					<p class="prose-body mt-7">
						Write me who pays, who is paid, who takes a cut and when the work counts as delivered.
						I answer the same working day, in writing. Free, and no call.
					</p>
					<p class="stamp mt-9">The facts on this page were checked at the source on {verifiedOn}</p>
				</div>
				<div class="md:col-span-4 md:col-start-9 md:self-start md:justify-self-end">
					<a href={paths.contact.en} class="btn btn-primary">Send me the flow</a>
					<p class="mt-6 text-sm text-paper/70">
						Or read <a href={paths.marketplace.en} class="link">what I build</a> for marketplaces.
					</p>
				</div>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>
</Site>
